WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:11.370
This is the debrief for The Messy Export, where one function, prepare_enrollment(), turns a raw CSV export into an analysis-ready data frame.

2
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.930
First, read every column as text, so nothing is guessed for you.

3
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.170
Second, repair dates, sites, and arms with explicit rules.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.150
Third, return one clean row per participant, in a stable order.

5
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:30.400
Line 2 reads every column as character, trims whitespace, and treats empty strings and "NA" as missing.

6
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.700
Then unique() drops the exact duplicate rows the export contains.

7
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.540
Dates arrive in three formats, so parse them in passes.

8
00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:43.700
Try %Y-%m-%d first, then fill only the values still missing, first with %m/%d/%Y, and finally with %d %b %Y.

9
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.630
Now build the output with an explicit type for every column.

10
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.070
Site codes are trimmed, upper-cased, and given their hyphen by one regular expression.

11
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.890
The arm column is cleaned and turned into a factor with fixed levels: Placebo, then Treatment.

12
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.070
Finally, sort by participant_id, so the same input always gives the same row order.

13
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.230
Three mistakes account for most failed attempts.

14
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.590
Parsing every date with one format turns the other two formats into NA, and the dates check fails.

15
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.740
Leaving empty strings as "" instead of NA fails the values check.

16
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:23.850
And anything hard-coded, like particular IDs or a row count, passes on the sample but fails on a second export it has never seen.

17
00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.900
This solution passes all seven checks.

18
00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:33.900
The validator self-test runs this exact code against every one of them, so what you see here is what the boss grades.

19
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.880
Your own solution can look different.

20
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.260
What matters is that it passes the same checks.
